the gate has no test-set diff: a reconciling suite total cannot see an even swap, and the ad-hoc log-scraping check is itself defeated by parallel-test interleaving

THE INCIDENT (lane-enum, B158, 2026-09-01). A test insertion landed between
`#[test]` and an existing test. That silently disabled
`hover_rejects_nadeo_class_storage_key` and double-registered one of the new
tests. THE SUITE TOTAL RECONCILED EXACTLY -- +1 duplicate and -1 disabled
cancelled -- so the delta arithmetic every lane in this batch relies on produced
a clean, correct-looking green over a disabled test. Nothing in the suite could
see it. `cargo clippy` caught it, via `dead_code` + `duplicate_macro_attributes`.

WHY THE GATE CANNOT SEE IT. Counting cannot distinguish a set from its
cardinality. Any even-numbered swap is invisible to a total, and the cheapest
instance -- one disabled while one is added -- is also the likeliest, because it
is exactly what a misplaced insertion produces by default.

And the exactness is the hazard, not the miss: a total that came out NEAR the
expected number would have been investigated. Coming out precisely right retired
the question. This defect gets worse as arithmetic discipline improves. It
defeats lanes that reconcile carefully and leaves sloppy ones untouched.

WHAT TO BUILD. A test-SET diff in the gate tooling, reported alongside the
SUITE-VERIFIED line: names added, names removed, against the merge base. A
removal that the lane did not intend is the alarm; today nothing raises it.

USE `--list`, NOT A PARSED RUN LOG. This is the load-bearing design decision and
it comes from the ad-hoc check failing in exactly this way. lane-enum's first
set diff reported 10 added, including a test in a file it never touched: a stderr
warning from a parallel test had interleaved into an `... ok` line and defeated
its `^test` anchor. The polarity is what makes it dangerous rather than merely
ironic -- that interleave produced a phantom ADDITION, which is confusing; had it
hit one of the lane's own lines it would have reported a test REMOVED, which is
the alarm a lane would have believed and acted on.

Each test binary's `--list` output is not interleaved with test-body output and
needs no `--test-threads=1`, so it removes the interleaving exposure at the
source instead of hardening a regex against it. Do not parse the run log.

REQUIRED EVIDENCE, run not described:
  - RED: reproduce the B158 shape on a scratch branch -- move a `#[test]` so one
    test is disabled and one double-registered. The total must reconcile, and
    the current gate must report green. Capture that.
  - GREEN: the set diff names the removed test.
  - AND THE INSTRUMENT MUST BE PROVEN ABLE TO SEE IT BEFORE ITS ZERO COUNTS:
    feed it a known-disabled test and require it to say so. A set check that has
    never reported a removal is in the same position as the suite total it is
    meant to backstop. See docs/verification-hazards.md, "A control proves
    nothing by not failing".
  - POSITIVE CONTROL: an ordinary lane branch that only ADDS tests reports its
    additions and zero removals, so the check is not simply always-alarming.

Related: B162 (a filter matching nothing reports green) is the same loss reached
from the other end -- that one keeps the assertion and removes the population,
this one removes the assertion and keeps the population, and both then report a
zero indistinguishable from a real one. Both belong to lane-tooling; do B162
first, it is smaller and its positive-control discipline is the same.

MEASURED ON THIS TREE (lane-unused, 2026-09-01). Three findings; the third is a
constraint, not a detail.

1. FORMAT. `--list` emits `<name>: test` per line, a blank line, then
   `N tests, M benchmarks`. Lib tests are fully module-qualified
   (`analysis::tests::analyze_parses_simple_function`); integration tests are
   bare. Confirmed not interleaved with test-body output.

2. `#[ignore]` IS A BLIND SPOT AND IT IS CHEAPLY CLOSABLE. Ignored tests still
   appear in the plain `--list` -- verified: `config::tests::unused_callbacks_
   load_wiring_child` appears in both `--list` and `--list --ignored`. So a name-
   set diff catches the B158 defect (the attribute is swallowed, the function
   stops being a test, it vanishes from the list) but would NOT catch a test
   disabled by adding `#[ignore]`. That is a real way to disable a test and we
   have 12 ignored already.

   Compare TWO sets: `--list` and `--list --ignored`. A test going active ->
   ignored is then unchanged in the first and newly present in the second.
   Whichever way this is resolved, state the blindness explicitly in the tool's
   own output or docs: a check whose gap is undocumented gets trusted past its
   range, which is how the suite total came to be trusted as a set check.

3. THE BINARY-ENUMERATION STEP IS ITSELF A NOTHING-RAN ZERO, AND IT WAS HIT
   WHILE MEASURING THIS TICKET. A first probe selected
   `target/debug/deps/openplanet_lsp-d408a682e854e302`, ran `--list`, and got
   0 tests -- one step from the conclusion "the lib harness lists nothing". The
   real harness was `-418c7f45a6ea779a` with 2176. `deps/` holds several
   artifacts matching a crate name: stale harnesses, rlibs, non-test
   executables. A glob or a newest-first pick can silently select one listing
   zero.

   SO: take binary paths from `cargo test --no-run --message-format=json`, which
   names each executable exactly. NEVER from a `deps/` glob.

   The reason this is a hard constraint and not a preference: a set diff over
   zero binaries reports zero removals and reconciles perfectly. The instrument
   built to backstop a vacuous check would acquire a vacuous first step -- the
   exact recursion this ticket exists to stop, arriving in the replacement.

   It was caught the same way as B162's original: the count was wrong for a
   reason the output did not state. A 0 from a binary that should hold thousands
   is only suspicious if you already know the denominator you expected.

None of this weakens the `--list` design -- 2 and 3 are both fixable at the
source rather than by hardening a parser, which is the argument for it.